# Security, Testing, and Final Review

## Auth and security

Handle auth-sensitive work server-side. Never rely only on frontend route protection. Always verify authorization on the server.

Do not expose sensitive data or private fields to Client Components unless needed.

Never trust client-provided user IDs, roles, permissions, prices, ownership, or subscription status.

Never hardcode secrets, expose tokens, commit credentials, trust client input, disable security checks, or bypass auth. Do not render unsanitized HTML without understanding XSS risk.

Use env vars for secrets. Never expose server-only env vars or private credentials in Client Components.

## Bug prevention

Verify the change does not break existing features, auth, navigation, APIs, state, forms, layouts, permissions, SEO, caching, or rendering.

For every bug: find root cause, fix the underlying problem (not a symptom patch), check related paths and regressions, then verify.

Do not use hacks unless there is genuinely no better solution.

## Edge cases

Consider null/undefined, empty arrays/strings, invalid IDs and input, slow network, failed APIs, expired/unauthorized auth, missing permissions, duplicate submit/requests, large datasets, mobile/screen sizes, refresh, back button, stale data. Do not assume ideal input.

## Performance

Make it correct and maintainable first. Then check unnecessary renders, expensive work, large lists, duplicate APIs, waterfalls, large client bundles/Client Components, unnecessary effects/state, images, lazy loading, code splitting.

Do not blindly use `useMemo`, `useCallback`, or `React.memo` without a reason.

## Production quality

Do not leave `console.log`, debug/hack/commented-out/dead code, unused vars/imports, fake data, hardcoded credentials, unnecessary TODOs, TypeScript errors, warnings, or broken imports/links.

## Testing

Before considering a task complete, run the project's existing lint, typecheck, unit/integration/E2E tests, and production build as appropriate. Do not ignore test or TypeScript failures. "It compiles" does not mean "it works." Add tests for critical untested business rules when appropriate.

If the change is UI (layout, styling, routing, client state, rendered data), verify in the browser end-to-end — not just a screenshot.

## Next.js architecture check

- Page is a Server Component unless client is required; `"use client"` is as small as possible.
- Initial data fetched server-side; no duplicate or sequential-independent requests.
- SSG/ISR/SSR and cache/revalidation chosen intentionally.
- Components small and reused; no unnecessary abstraction.

## Final review (mandatory)

Review the complete change as a senior engineer reviewing someone else's PR:

- Correctness, edge cases, unexpected failures
- Clean code, small units, clear names, no extra complexity
- Reused existing code; no duplicate logic; no needless abstractions
- Follows existing architecture; server/client split correct
- Server-first fetching; correct SSG/ISR/SSR/cache
- No `any`/unsafe casts; typed APIs; null handled
- No extra renders, waterfalls, or oversized Client Components
- Secrets, server authz, input validation, sensitive data
- Loading/error/empty/responsive/accessible
- Cleanup: logs, unused imports, dead/temp code

Fix all errors introduced by the change. Do not mark complete until reviewed and verified.
